I now have a complete and definitive picture from multiple sources. Here is the full answer:

---

## `app.graphToPrompt()` — The Definitive Reference

### The Method

```javascript
const result = await app.graphToPrompt();
```

It is an **async method on the global `app` object**. Call it with no arguments from any custom node extension or browser console.

---

### What It Returns

A Promise that resolves to an object with **two keys**:

```javascript
{
  output:   { ... },   // API format — what you send to the server as "prompt"
  workflow: { ... }    // UI format — what you save to disk as a .json file
}
```

**`result.output`** — the API/prompt format you need. It is a flat object keyed by string node IDs:

```json
{
  "1": {
    "class_type": "CheckpointLoaderSimple",
    "inputs": {
      "ckpt_name": "v1-5-pruned-emaonly.ckpt"
    },
    "_meta": { "title": "Load Checkpoint" }
  },
  "3": {
    "class_type": "KSampler",
    "inputs": {
      "seed": 123456,
      "steps": 20,
      "cfg": 7.0,
      "sampler_name": "euler",
      "scheduler": "normal",
      "model": ["1", 0],
      "positive": ["6", 0],
      "negative": ["7", 0],
      "latent_image": ["5", 0]
    },
    "_meta": { "title": "KSampler" }
  }
}
```

- **Literal inputs** are strings/numbers/booleans directly
- **Connection inputs** are `[source_node_id_string, output_slot_index_integer]` arrays
- Nodes with `mode === NEVER` (bypassed/disabled) are excluded
- Virtual nodes (reroutes, primitives) are flattened/resolved before return
- Each node entry also contains `_meta: { title: "..." }` with the display name

**`result.workflow`** — the LiteGraph UI format (has `nodes`, `links`, `groups`, `extra`, `version` etc.). This is what `.json` export saves. You generally don't need this for API submission.

---

### How the Queue Button Uses It

From the compiled `api-z_dXWY_S.js`, `api.queuePrompt()` destructures the return value directly:

```javascript
// Inside app's queuePrompt method:
let t = await this.graphToPrompt(this.rootGraph);
// ...then calls:
await G.queuePrompt(e, t, { partialExecutionTargets: n, previewMethod: o });

// And inside api.queuePrompt(position, graphToPromptResult, options):
async queuePrompt(e, t, n) {
  let { output: r, workflow: i } = t;   // <-- destructures output and workflow
  const body = {
    client_id: this.clientId ?? '',
    prompt: r,                           // output goes here as "prompt"
    extra_data: {
      extra_pnginfo: { workflow: i }     // workflow embedded in extra_data
    }
  };
  // POSTs to /prompt endpoint
}
```

So the server receives `prompt` (= `result.output`) and `extra_data.extra_pnginfo.workflow` (= `result.workflow`).

---

### Internal Implementation (from compiled `dialogService-Cr3DyAja.js`)

The decompiled core logic (reformatted for readability):

```javascript
graphToPrompt = async (graph, options = {}) => {
  const { sortNodes = false } = options;

  // 1. Apply virtual nodes (primitives link their values into the graph)
  for (let node of graph.computeExecutionOrder(false)) {
    const innerNodes = node.getInnerNodes ? node.getInnerNodes(new Map) : [node];
    for (let n of innerNodes) {
      if (n.isVirtualNode) n.applyToGraph?.();
    }
  }

  // 2. Serialize to workflow (UI) format
  const r = graph.serialize({ sortNodes });
  // Strip localized names
  for (let node of r.nodes) {
    for (let input of node.inputs ?? []) delete input.localized_name;
    for (let output of node.outputs ?? []) delete output.localized_name;
  }
  compressWidgetInputSlots(r);
  r.extra ??= {};
  r.extra.frontendVersion = `1.38.13`;

  // 3. Build execution order map (handles group nodes)
  const nodeMap = new Map();
  for (let node of graph.computeExecutionOrder(false)) {
    const dto = isGroupNode(node)
      ? new ExecutableGroupNodeDTO(node, [], nodeMap)
      : new Ni(node, [], nodeMap);
    for (let n of dto.getInnerNodes()) nodeMap.set(n.id, n);
    nodeMap.set(dto.id, dto);
  }

  // 4. Build API output format
  const output = {};
  for (let node of nodeMap.values()) {
    if (node.isVirtualNode || node.mode === NEVER || node.mode === BYPASS) continue;

    const inputs = {};

    // Serialize widget values
    for (let [idx, widget] of node.widgets.entries()) {
      if (!widget.name || widget.options?.serialize === false) continue;
      const value = widget.serializeValue
        ? await widget.serializeValue(node, idx)
        : widget.value;
      inputs[widget.name] = Array.isArray(value) ? { __value__: value } : value;
    }

    // Serialize connections
    for (let [idx, input] of node.inputs.entries()) {
      const resolved = node.resolveInput(idx);
      if (resolved) {
        if (resolved.widgetInfo) {
          const { value } = resolved.widgetInfo;
          inputs[input.name] = Array.isArray(value) ? { __value__: value } : value;
        } else {
          inputs[input.name] = [String(resolved.origin_id), parseInt(resolved.origin_slot)];
        }
      }
    }

    output[String(node.id)] = {
      inputs,
      class_type: node.comfyClass,
      _meta: { title: node.title }
    };
  }

  // 5. Clean up dangling connections (links to nodes not in output)
  for (let { inputs } of Object.values(output)) {
    for (let [key, val] of Object.entries(inputs)) {
      if (Array.isArray(val) && val.length === 2 && !output[val[0]]) {
        delete inputs[key];
      }
    }
  }

  return { workflow: r, output };
};
```

---

### Summary — Exact Calls You Need

| Goal | Call |
|------|------|
| Get API-format prompt (to send to server) | `const { output } = await app.graphToPrompt()` |
